# Running the Rivet CRAB submissions

This repository contains the source files needed for the four submissions,
plus optional event-weight tools. It is an overlay for CMSSW_14_2_0/src,
not a full CMSSW checkout.
Run submission commands from this directory: the scripts use relative paths.

## Required files

Keep the submission script and the files in its row:

| Submission script | Directly required files |
| --- | --- |
| crab_submit_GEN-25-002_DY.py | rivet_GEN-25-002_DY_cfg.py |
| crab_submit_GEN-25-002_tt.py | rivet_GEN-25-002_tt_cfg.py |
| crab_submit_GEN-25-002_W.py | rivet_GEN-25-002_W_cfg.py |
| crab_submit_NonIsoPheno.py | rivet_NonIsoPheno_cfg.py, run_rivet_crab.sh |

Additional software/source dependencies:

- A compatible CMSSW installation, CRABClient/CRABAPI, and CMS grid credentials.
- GeneratorInterface/RivetInterface/: keep its source, interface, python,
  data, and BuildFile.xml files to rebuild the local Rivet interface. The
  configurations use RivetAnalyzer, MergedGenParticleProducer, and
  GenParticles2HepMCConverter.
- Rivet/: selected CMS analysis metadata/reference data and rivetSetup.sh.
  The CMSSW release supplies the requested CMS, ATLAS, and MC analysis
  implementations and external libraries.
- NonIsoPheno additionally needs the custom RivetNonIsoPheno analysis.
  RivetAnalysis/RivetNonIsoPheno.cc and .info provide the standalone plugin
  route described below.
  Rebuild the .so in the same CMSSW environment used by the jobs; an existing
  binary from another environment may be incompatible.

MyTools/Test/plugins/ is included for optional weight handling; see below.
Only requested CMS analysis metadata/reference data are included under
Rivet/SMP and Rivet/TOP. All nine corresponding sources were marked SKIP_FILES
in the original checkout because CMSSW already supplies their implementations.
Unrelated packages, duplicate custom-analysis sources, binaries, logs, CRAB
work directories, and local event/output files are excluded.

## Install into CMSSW

On a CMS host with CVMFS, create a fresh release and copy this overlay into src:

```bash
source /cvmfs/cms.cern.ch/cmsset_default.sh
scram project CMSSW CMSSW_14_2_0
git clone https://github.com/joonblee/cmssw-rivet.git
cp -a cmssw-rivet/. CMSSW_14_2_0/src/
cd CMSSW_14_2_0/src
```

## Set up and compile

On a CERN/CMS host with CVMFS, start in CMSSW_14_2_0/src:

```bash
source /cvmfs/cms.cern.ch/cmsset_default.sh
eval "$(scram runtime -sh)"
scram b -j 4
source "$CMSSW_BASE/src/Rivet/rivetSetup.sh"
source /cvmfs/cms.cern.ch/crab3/crab.sh
voms-proxy-init --voms cms
```

The Python submission/configuration files do not need compilation. `scram b`
builds the local CMSSW packages and Rivet analyses using their BuildFile.xml
files. Check that the build finishes successfully before submitting.
Re-source the Rivet setup in each new shell. It adds this checkout's analysis
metadata/reference-data directories to Rivet's search paths.

For the standalone NonIsoPheno plugin, from src:

```bash
rivet-build RivetNonIsoPheno.so RivetAnalysis/RivetNonIsoPheno.cc
export RIVET_ANALYSIS_PATH="$PWD${RIVET_ANALYSIS_PATH:+:$RIVET_ANALYSIS_PATH}"
export RIVET_INFO_PATH="$PWD/RivetAnalysis${RIVET_INFO_PATH:+:$RIVET_INFO_PATH}"
rivet --list-analyses | rg RivetNonIsoPheno
```

For DY/W/tt, use `rivet --list-analyses` to check that all analysis names in the
corresponding configuration are available. NonIsoPheno also requests MC_XS
and HiggsTemplateCrossSections.

## NonIsoPheno worker plugin

Build RivetNonIsoPheno.so with the standalone command above before submission.
The repository's NonIsoPheno script includes the wrapper, plugin, and .info in
JobType.inputFiles. CRAB sends them to the worker sandbox; run_rivet_crab.sh
adds the working directory to RIVET_ANALYSIS_PATH and runs
`cmsRun -j FrameworkJobReport.xml PSet.py`. CRAB generates PSet.py.
The .so is intentionally excluded from Git and must be rebuilt after cloning.

## Submit to CRAB

Review the active datasets, requestName, RunDate/workArea, and storageSite
in each script before running. The current storage site is T3_KR_KNU.
Use a fresh RunDate/workArea or unique request names when repeating submissions.

Run the desired command(s) from src:

```bash
python3 crab_submit_GEN-25-002_DY.py
python3 crab_submit_GEN-25-002_tt.py
python3 crab_submit_GEN-25-002_W.py
python3 crab_submit_NonIsoPheno.py
```

These commands submit real jobs. Each script calls crabCommand('submit') for
its uncommented dataset blocks, potentially creating several tasks.
Use Python execution for this multi-dataset workflow: requestName/inputDataset
are filled inside the __main__ block, so `crab submit -c <script>` is not an
equivalent invocation.

DY/W/tt request maxEvents=-1 and one input file per job. NonIsoPheno currently
runs a test: maxEvents=10000, unitsPerJob=1, totalUnits=1.
All four request output.yoda as an output file. DY also requests
output.weights.json and enables stored LHE variations; see the theory workflow below.

Monitor a task using its actual directory printed by submission:

```bash
crab status -d crablogRUNDATE/crab_REQUEST_NAME
crab getlog -d crablogRUNDATE/crab_REQUEST_NAME
crab getoutput -d crablogRUNDATE/crab_REQUEST_NAME
```

For NonIsoPheno the work-area prefix is crablog_NonIsoPheno, rather than crablog.

## Optional local run

CRAB fills the otherwise empty input list in the DY/W/tt configurations.
For a local cmsRun test, provide a readable MiniAOD file in
process.source.fileNames first. NonIsoPheno already contains a local DY file
path; replace it if you want to test the QCD dataset used by its submission.

After setup/build and, for NonIsoPheno, the plugin search-path exports above:

```bash
cmsRun rivet_GEN-25-002_DY_cfg.py maxEvents=100 yodafile=DY_test.yoda
cmsRun rivet_GEN-25-002_tt_cfg.py maxEvents=100 yodafile=tt_test.yoda
cmsRun rivet_GEN-25-002_W_cfg.py maxEvents=100 yodafile=W_test.yoda
cmsRun rivet_NonIsoPheno_cfg.py maxEvents=100 yodafile=NonIsoPheno_test.yoda
```

Validation of the original overlay: the exported GeneratorInterface/RivetInterface and MyTools packages
compiled successfully in a fresh CMSSW_14_2_0 release. The standalone
RivetNonIsoPheno plugin also built successfully. The exported HTXS analysis
uses RIVET_DECLARE_PLUGIN so it registers with Rivet 4. No CRAB jobs were submitted
and no dataset-level physics validation was performed.

## Optional MyTools event weights

Files: MyTools/Test/plugins/HepMCWeightUnityProducer.cc,
HepMCWeightFilter.cc, and BuildFile.xml. They compile with the same `scram b`
command above. This repository uses edm::HepMC3Product, matching the supplied
GenParticles2HepMCConverter and RivetAnalyzer. The original workspace tools
used HepMC2 products and could not connect to this HepMC3 chain directly.

These tools modify/select existing generator weights; they do not calculate
new matrix-element, PDF, or scale weights. The converter copies weights from
GenEventInfoProduct (the input `generator` product) into the HepMC event.

Choose one of these configurations in a rivet_*_cfg.py, replacing its existing
process.p assignment. The default submission files keep original weights.

### Replace the nominal event weight with one

```python
process.weightUnity = cms.EDProducer(
    'HepMCWeightUnityProducer',
    src=cms.InputTag('generator', 'unsmeared'),
)
process.rivetAnalyzer.HepMCCollection = cms.InputTag('weightUnity')
process.p = cms.Path(
    process.mergedGenParticles * process.generator *
    process.weightUnity * process.rivetAnalyzer
)
```

The producer reconstructs a separate HepMC3 event and sets weights[0] to 1.0
(or creates it if the vector is empty). Additional weights are unchanged.
Negative nominal weights also become +1; weighted NLO normalization therefore
changes. To analyze only the nominal weight, use
`process.rivetAnalyzer.skipMultiWeights = cms.bool(True)`.
It does not modify the separate GenEventInfoProduct.

### Reject events with a large absolute nominal weight

```python
process.weightFilter = cms.EDFilter(
    'HepMCWeightFilter',
    src=cms.InputTag('generator', 'unsmeared'),
    maxWeight=cms.double(100.0),
)
process.rivetAnalyzer.HepMCCollection = cms.InputTag('generator', 'unsmeared')
process.p = cms.Path(
    process.mergedGenParticles * process.generator *
    process.weightFilter * process.rivetAnalyzer
)
```

The filter accepts abs(weights[0]) < maxWeight. Equality is rejected; an empty
weight vector is treated as weight 1.0. Surviving weights stay unchanged.
Use a positive threshold appropriate for the sample. Filtering changes the
selected sample and its normalization. The commented filter example inherited
from the workspace used generator::GEN; use generator:unsmeared as above,
since this filter consumes the converted HepMC event.

### Validate weight behavior

Use a small readable MiniAOD input and run the optional local commands first.
Compare the baseline and modified output.yoda histograms/counters, including
MC_XS: unity weights should change nominal weighted sums; the filter should
remove events at/above the selected absolute-weight threshold. Alternative
weight streams are preserved unless skipMultiWeights is enabled.

## DY theory uncertainties

The DY configuration now imports stored LHE weights and retains stored generator
weights, including PS variations when the sample contains them. CRAB transfers
output.yoda plus output.weights.json. The sidecar retains exact LHE IDs, raw XML
weight definitions and generator descriptions. Variation names are now
LHE_<id> and GEN_<index>_<cleaned description>; nominal is still the empty name.
The shared analyser finalises once per job (rather than once per run), matches
LHE values by ID, and rejects inconsistent layouts across input events/runs.
The indexed generator names also apply to new W/tt/NonIsoPheno runs using this
interface. Do not mix old/new outputs in one merge.

Rebuild the interface with `scram b -j 4` before a local test or CRAB submission.
The default LHE label is externalLHEProducer; source is tried as a fallback.
Override the primary label with `lheCollection=<label>` if necessary. Samples
without LHE weights are still analysed; the metadata marks LHE unavailable.
To require LHE weights in a local configuration, set
`process.rivetAnalyzer.allowMissingLHEWeights = cms.bool(False)`.
Weights that were not generated/stored cannot be reconstructed by this workflow.
Existing nominal-only YODA files need a new event-analysis run; no event
generation is needed if the required weights exist in the input MiniAOD.

The default preserveVariationRate=True explicitly passes the nominal cross
section to Rivet's scalar API before finalisation, so
sigma_i = sigma_nom * sumW_i / sumW_nom. Rivet 4 also rederives variation cross
sections during finalisation; this makes the intended rate convention explicit
in the interface and covers raw output when DoFinalize=False.
The central cross section is unchanged. Analyses that explicitly normalise
distributions to unit area still produce shape uncertainties, as intended.

### Merge and calculate

Work in the same CMSSW/Rivet environment used for the jobs. Python must be able
to import yoda; automatic PDF treatment also requires lhapdf and the relevant
installed set metadata. Keep ONE sample's downloaded job outputs in each folder.
Use Rivet's equivalent-sample merge (`-e`), which merges raw accumulators and
reruns finalisation; adding already normalised curves with yodamerge is not the
equivalent operation. Check rivet-merge's log for skipped non-reentrant analyses.

```bash
scram b -j 4
cmsRun rivet_GEN-25-002_DY_cfg.py maxEvents=100 yodafile=DY_test.yoda

# After downloading the outputs from a new CRAB task:
rivet-merge -e -o DY_merged.yoda downloaded_DY/output_*.yoda
python3 theory_uncertainties.py --metadata downloaded_DY/*.json --list
python3 theory_uncertainties.py DY_merged.yoda \
    --metadata downloaded_DY/*.json --output DY_theory
rivet-mkhtml DY_theory.yoda
```

The local cmsRun example requires an input file in process.source.fileNames.
Use a new RunDate/workArea before resubmitting CRAB. This code change does not
submit jobs. Pass ALL metadata sidecars for the sample; the script checks that
their definitions agree. It cannot verify that you downloaded all CRAB jobs.

If several complete PDF ensembles are present, select the one used for the
nominal production with `--pdf-set <LHAPDF-set-name>`. This option also declares
that the nominal histogram is member 0 if that member's weight was not stored.
The script never assumes that a numerical LHE ID denotes a particular PDF member.
Do not select an alternative PDF family and interpret its error as the nominal
production PDF uncertainty without checking the corresponding central shift.

Output files:

- DY_theory.json: central values, MC statistical errors, separate absolute
  down/up uncertainty components, total theory errors, availability and skipped
  object types. LHAPDF's replica central values are also recorded for comparison
  while the plotted curve retains the generator's nominal prediction.
- DY_theory.csv: the same binwise central values and separate error components.
- DY_theory.yoda: nominal curves with a combined theory band at the original
  analysis paths, suitable for rivet-mkhtml. Add `--include-stat` to include
  nominal MC statistical errors in the plotted band as well.

### Prescriptions and incomplete inputs

- ME scales: the conventional 7-point envelope (nominal plus six variations
  with muR/muF factors 0.5, 1, 2, excluding 0.5/2 and 2/0.5). Automatic mode
  requires a unique complete group and prefers explicit LHE streams over copies
  carried in generator weights.
- PDF: LHAPDF's 68.268949% CL prescription, using complete, correctly ordered
  members. Replica and symmetric/asymmetric Hessian representations are
  supported. Distinct PDF ensembles are alternatives and are not added together.
- alpha_s: LHAPDF's separate parameter component for a supported PDF+alphas
  set (including the legacy +as spelling), counted once. For a unique explicitly labelled standalone pair, use half
  the absolute difference. This uses the stored alpha_s step: check that it
  matches the intended uncertainty (commonly +/-0.001 about the central value).
- PS: separate inclusive ISR and FSR envelopes using muR factors 0.5 and 2
  when their descriptions/standard CMS aliases identify them. Reduced,
  conservative and splitting-kernel variations are retained in YODA but are
  not silently mixed into these envelopes. Tune, hadronisation and matching
  uncertainties require their own samples or prescriptions.
- The combined theory band adds the available independent components in
  quadrature, separately for up/down. This is an explicit plotting convention,
  not a statistical confidence interval for missing higher orders; use the
  separate components for interpretations with other correlation assumptions.

Missing or ambiguous groups are reported as unavailable, never zero uncertainty.
For a strict production check, add
`--require scale pdf alphas` (and `isr fsr` if those weights are expected).
By default a partial band contains the available components and records all
missing components. Missing histogram streams, incompatible binning, malformed
headers and non-finite bins fail with an error rather than an invented value.
Histo1D, Profile1D, Estimate1D, their numeric discrete-bin equivalents and
Scatter2D are processed; unsupported dimensions
and counters are listed in the report, and /RAW, /REF and /TMP objects are excluded.

In particular, NNPDF31_nnlo_as_0118_mc_hessian_pdfas stores member 101 at
alpha_s(MZ)=0.116 and member 102 at 0.120, i.e. +/-0.002. For a +/-0.001
uncertainty, add `--alphas-rescale 0.5` under a linear-response assumption.
The default factor is 1 and uses the actual stored variation step; this factor
is recorded in the output group definitions. It applies to both combined
PDF+alpha_s sets and standalone pairs. Source set metadata:
https://lhapdfsets.web.cern.ch/current/NNPDF31_nnlo_as_0118_mc_hessian_pdfas/NNPDF31_nnlo_as_0118_mc_hessian_pdfas.info

For generators with non-standard descriptions, provide `--groups groups.json`.
It replaces automatic grouping and uses exact names from `--list`. For example
(replace every illustrative name with the actual weight name):

```json
{
  "scale": {"method": "envelope", "weights": ["SCALE_1", "SCALE_2", "SCALE_3", "SCALE_4", "SCALE_5", "SCALE_6"]},
  "pdf": {"method": "symmhessian", "weights": ["PDF_EV1", "PDF_EV2"], "central": "", "confidence_level": 68.26894921370858},
  "alphas": {"method": "half_difference", "weights": ["ALPHAS_DOWN", "ALPHAS_UP"], "rescale": 1.0},
  "isr": {"method": "envelope", "weights": ["ISR_DOWN", "ISR_UP"]},
  "fsr": {"method": "envelope", "weights": ["FSR_DOWN", "FSR_UP"]}
}
```

The example's PDF member count is illustrative, not a valid ensemble to copy
blindly. `replicas` excludes the central member and uses the sample STD about
the replica mean. `symmhessian` uses one variation per eigenvector; `hessian`
uses ordered +/- pairs and gives separate up/down errors. Set confidence_level
to the source Hessian CL (e.g. 90) for conversion to one sigma. A manifest group
with method `lhapdf`, set `<name>` and a full member-ordered weights list delegates
to LHAPDF and separates alpha_s automatically. `rescale` lets a reviewed
standalone alpha_s pair be converted to a different step under a linear-response
assumption. The script rejects unknown names and reusing a variation in two groups.

References: LHAPDF PDFSet::uncertainty and error-set metadata conventions:
https://www.lhapdf.org/classLHAPDF_1_1PDFSet.html
https://www.lhapdf.org/config.html
Rivet 4 multiweight plotting:
https://gitlab.com/hepcedar/rivet/-/blob/rivet-4.0.1/doc/tutorials/multiweights_plotting.md
CMS standard Pythia PS weight definitions:
https://github.com/cms-sw/cmssw/blob/CMSSW_14_2_0/Configuration/Generator/python/PSweightsPythia/PythiaPSweightsSettings_cfi.py

### Validation

```bash
python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -I. tests/test_weight_utils.cc -o /tmp/test_rivet_weight_utils
/tmp/test_rivet_weight_utils
```

The Python suite includes a native YODA round-trip/bin-width test, run when
the bindings are available, and analytical checks for replicas, Hessian CL
conversion, scale-point selection, PS grouping, component combination and
invalid inputs. The standalone C++ test covers signed event reweighting,
reordered/missing/duplicate LHE IDs and metadata escaping. A full CMSSW rebuild
and a small real-input cmsRun test are still required on a CMS host before large
production; this revision has not submitted CRAB tasks or validated actual datasets.
